    "lawCode": "Amd §2018-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2038A\n\nSPONSOR: CARLUCCI\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\napplications for absentee ballots for school district elections\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to amend provisions relating to the\ninformation which must be included on applications for absentee ballots\nfor school district elections.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 Subdivision 2 of section 2018-a of the Education Law is\namended. 2.a. A qualified voter may vote as an absentee voter under this\nsection if during all the hours of voting on the day of an election if\nthey are: absent from the county of residence, ill or physically disa-\nbled, caring for an ill or disabled individual, hospitalized, an inmate,\na patient of a veteran's administration hospital, detained in jail\nawaiting action by a grand jury or awaiting trial, or in jail or prison\nfor an offense other than a felony, provided they are still qualified to\nvote in the election district of their residence. b. Each person who\ndesires and is entitled to vote absentee must present a form prescribed\nby the state board of elections to the district clerk. c.  When filed,\nthe application for an absentee ballot must contain: (1) Applicant's\nfull name, date of birth, and address (2) A statement that the applicant\nis re qualified and registered voter (3) a statement, as appropriate,\nthat on the day of such election the applicant expects in good faith to\nbe unable to vote in person because of a reason listed in '2.a' of this\nact. d. Provides a statement that must be declared with the signature of\nthe voter. e. An applicant who is a qualified voter who is substantially\nimpaired and whose registration record has been marked 'permanently\ndisabled' by the BOE is entitled to receive and absentee ballot without\nmaking a separate application for such ballot.  f. An application must\nbe received by the district clerk no earlier than the 30 day before the\nelection. If the application requests that the absentee ballot be\nmailed, such application must be received not later than 7 days before\nthe election. If the application is delivered to the district clerk in\nperson, it must be received no later than the day before the election.\ng. Prior to 6 days before the election, the district clerk shall send by\nregular mail an absentee ballot to each qualified applicant from which\nan application was received. If the applicant hand delivers the applica-\ntions within 1 - 6 days prior to the election, and if qualified, the\ndistrict clerk should give them an absentee ballot.\n\nSection 2. Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nThis bill was amended to account for duplicated, redundant language.\nSubstantially, it remains unchanged.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Election Law was amended in 2010 simplifying the absentee ballot\napplication for registered voters. The new simplified absentee ballot\napplication needs to be enacted in Education Law so that the absentee\nballot applications for school district elections are the same.\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA. 2840 and S. 1058 of 2017/2018\nA. 5031 and S. 847 of 2015/2016\nA. 5081 and S. 3552 of 2013/2014\nA. 2521-B and S. 1553-B of 2011/2012\nA. 4315-A and S. 3253-A of 2009/2010\nA. 5451 of 2007/2008\nA. 4477 and S. 5049 of 2005/2006\nA. 6436 of 2003/2004\nA. 7887 of 2001/2002\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nOnce law, this legislation is expected to save monies associated with\nprinting, as the new absentee ballot applications will be shorter.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome law.\n",
